{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Lots.</b> They are known from the tradition of our ancestors. The Gaon [Saadia] said that Azazel is the name of a mountain, and it is so called because it is strong (עז), for the meaning of the [second part of the] name is like the meaning of 'mountains of El' (הררי אל) [in] 'Yekuti'el.' The Gaon and the Levi [R. Abraham ibn Ezra? or: the Levi] held that the alef in the word עזאזל is between zayin and zayin. Some say it is a mountain near Mount Sinai, and HaShem commanded to lead the goat and bring it up there until it is slaughtered, and afterward they would lead it through the Temple to another mountain — and the proof is 'Aaron did' [Leviticus 16:24?], and likewise at the festival of Shavuot, in my opinion, as I shall explain in its place. Some say that the worship of the Second Temple was not like the First, for the priest lacked garments and there is no atonement — and this commentator said that the goat is sent into the wilderness, for so it is written, 'and send the goat into the wilderness' [Leviticus 16:21?], and its meaning is like the meaning of the bird that purifies from leprosy, which is 'upon the face of the field' [Leviticus 14:7], a place where there is no settlement — and the proof: 'to a land cut off' [Leviticus 16:22]. But we have answered them: they send the goat into the wilderness as it is written, and they chase after it until it flees and goes up onto the rock; therefore the Sages, blessed be their memory, said 'and push him.' And R. Samuel said: even though it is written about the sin-offering goat (שעיר החטאת) that it is 'for the Name,' the sent goat (השעיר המשתלח) is also 'for the Name,' and there is no need, for the sender is not an offering, since it is not slaughtered. If you can grasp the sod that is after the word עזאזל, you will know its sod and the sod of its name, for it has companions in Scripture — and I shall reveal to you a little of the sod by hint: when you are thirty-three years old you will know it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "Resolved הגאון הלוי as two figures, 'the Gaon and the Levi,' following the reading's apparent logic; some witnesses read this as one epithet. The phrase 'the Levi' is left unresolved as the referent is uncertain. 'Push him' (ודוחפו) refers to the Mishnaic account of sending the goat over the cliff (Yoma 6:6), left untranslated beyond Ibn Ezra's terse reference. The 'thirty-three' hint is left cryptic per discipline."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:10:34.471088976Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}